-# Choosing citrus pectin for low-sugar gels: why matching DE is not enough
+# Low-sugar citrus pectin: match the setting window, calcium response and texture
 
-A low-sugar fruit spread and a calcium-containing yoghurt drink can both use pectin, but they do not ask it to do the same job. In one, a controlled gel network is the product; in the other, the priority may be keeping protein particles dispersed while preserving a drinkable texture. Commercial citrus pectins are offered specifically for these different functions.[11]
+Low-sugar fruit spreads need a gel that remains workable through filling and develops the intended texture on cooling. Acidified milk drinks often need protein particles to stay dispersed while the product remains drinkable. Commercial citrus pectin ranges include grades for both functions.[11]
 
-For a low-sugar gel, the useful purchasing question is therefore not simply **“Which LM pectin replaces this one?”** It is **“Which grade sets at the right point in our process, with our acid, calcium and solids, and gives the required texture after cooling?”**
+For a fruit-spread replacement, start with the final pH, calcium system, solids and temperature history. These give a supplier a more useful brief than an LM designation or a target DE alone.
 
-## HM, LM and amidated LM describe chemistry—not a complete application specification
+## Chemistry narrows the grade selection
 
-High-methoxyl pectin, or HM, has more than 50% of its carboxyl groups methyl-esterified; low-methoxyl pectin, or LM, has less than 50%. Amidated LM, often abbreviated LMA or LMAP, also contains amide groups, so its degree of amidation must be specified separately from its degree of methylation or esterification.[6][12]
+High-methoxyl pectin (HM) has more than 50% of its carboxyl groups methyl-esterified; low-methoxyl pectin (LM) has less than 50%. Amidated LM, also called LMA or LMAP, contains amide groups as well, so its degree of amidation (DA) is specified separately from its degree of methylation or esterification (DM or DE).[6][11]
 
-The broad distinction is useful: conventional HM gelation relies on a sufficiently acidic, high-solids environment, whereas calcium-mediated LM gelation allows substantially lower-solids products.[11][12] It does not follow that all LM grades need the same calcium addition, or that all LMA grades are interchangeable.
+Conventional HM gelation needs an acidic, high-soluble-solids environment. Calcium-mediated LM gelation permits lower-soluble-solids formulations.[11][12] Within either class, the supplier's application grade provides a more specific starting point.
 
