-## What a controlled citrus-pectin experiment adds
+## Similar methylation can produce different setting behaviour
 
-Kastner, Einhorn-Stoll and Drusch studied two commercially available citrus pectins: a non-amidated LM material with DM 30%, and an amidated LM material with DM 32% and DA 19%.[6] The nearly matched DM makes this more informative than a simple comparison between a high-DE jam grade and a low-DE gel grade.
+Kastner, Einhorn-Stoll and Drusch compared two commercially available citrus pectins: non-amidated LM with DM 30%, and amidated LM with DM 32% and DA 19%.[6] These were two different commercial materials with similar DM, not a matched pair differing only in amidation. Their Table 4 also reports different galacturonan contents.[6]
 
-The study used a sugar–acid model rather than a finished fruit spread. Its standard preparation contained 6 g pectin, sucrose, citric acid, sodium citrate and calcium chloride dihydrate, boiled to 900 g; total solids were maintained at 30 wt.%. The gels were prepared at least in triplicate, and the standard rheological cooling programme ran from 100°C to 10°C at 1 K/min.[6]
+The experiment used a sugar–acid model. The standard LM preparation contained 6 g pectin, sucrose, citric acid, sodium citrate and calcium chloride dihydrate, and was boiled down to 900 g. The authors report maintaining total solids at 30 wt.% across the systems and preparing gels at least in triplicate. The standard rheological cooling programme ran from 100°C to 10°C at 1 K/min.[6] Here, total solids is the paper's reported formulation measure; it is distinct from the supplier table's TSS and from a sugar-content measurement.
 
-Three observations matter for replacement decisions.
+Three findings help define a replacement trial.
 
-**First, equal methylation did not give equal setting behaviour.** In the authors' direct comparison at similar calcium-to-carboxyl ratios, the amidated material developed its main structure earlier and gave a more elastic, brittle gel, while the non-amidated material was softer and more viscous in character.[6] “Amidated” is therefore not a promise of a softer spread. The desired texture still needs to be defined and measured.
+**Setting time and final texture both changed.** At similar calcium-to-carboxyl ratios, the amidated material reached its main structure-development stage earlier during cooling. Its final response was more elastic; the authors described it as brittle, while describing the non-amidated material as softer and more viscous.[6] A spreadable texture therefore needs to be specified independently of an “amidated” designation.
 
-**Second, lowering pH did not move both materials in the same direction.** Across the acidic conditions discussed in the paper, non-amidated LM gels became more liquid-like as pH fell, whereas the amidated material became more solid-like. The authors attributed the difference to the declining contribution of calcium bridges and the additional contribution of hydrogen bonds involving amide groups.[6] Acid adjustment after a grade change is not just a flavour correction.
+**Acidification affected the two materials differently.** In the acidic range discussed in the paper, decreasing pH made the non-amidated gels more liquid-like and the amidated gels more solid-like. The authors attributed this to a declining contribution from calcium bridges and the additional hydrogen-bond contribution involving amide groups.[6] The same acid adjustment may change texture differently after a grade replacement.
 
-**Third, calcium changes the setting process, not merely the final firmness.** Increasing calcium in the amidated system raised the initial and critical structuring temperatures; at the highest tested calcium ratio, no clear classical gel point was detectable. The authors proposed that local microgels had formed rapidly before measurement began.[6] That finding makes early thickening or local gel formation a sensible observation in a plant trial, rather than judging a replacement only by next-day firmness.
+**Calcium affected the onset of setting.** In the amidated series, the initial structuring temperature rose across the tested calcium ratios. The critical structuring temperature rose initially, then fell slightly at the highest ratio. At that highest ratio, no clear classical gel point was detected; the authors proposed rapid local microgel formation before measurement as an explanation.[6] Early thickening and local particles are therefore useful observations during a process trial, alongside the cooled gel's texture.
 
-The experiment's calcium ratio was **R = 2[Ca²⁺]/[COO⁻]**, not milligrams of elemental calcium per serving.[6] It relates calcium to available dissociated carboxyl groups. Do not convert the paper into a universal calcium dose for every commercial powder: composition, ionisation and molecular structure are part of the comparison.
+The study expressed calcium loading as **R = 2[Ca²⁺]/[COO⁻]**, relating calcium to free carboxyl content.[6] Applying it to a commercial powder requires information about that material's composition and available carboxyl groups; it is not a ready-made elemental-calcium dose per serving.
 
-## “Calcium tolerant” is not “unaffected by calcium”
+## Calcium tolerance still has a working range
 
-Herbstreith & Fox describes amidated LM pectins as forming sufficiently firm gels with relatively small calcium quantities and allowing a wider calcium concentration range.[12] Its general brochure also warns that exceeding the calcium optimum can cause a brittle gel, syneresis or formation of insoluble calcium pectinate.[11]
+H&F describes amidated LM pectins as forming sufficiently firm gels with small calcium quantities and tolerating a wider calcium-concentration range.[12] Its general brochure warns that exceeding the calcium optimum can lead to brittle gels, syneresis or insoluble calcium pectinate.[11] It also lists low, medium, high and very high calcium reactivity among different amidated citrus grades.[11] A useful tolerance range must therefore be established for the chosen grade and formulation.
 
-These statements are compatible. A wider useful window does not remove its upper boundary. Nor is the window specified by DA alone: the same supplier lists low, medium, high and very high calcium reactivity among different amidated citrus grades.[11]
+The 2017 paper contains an important table–text discrepancy. In Table 2, increasing R from 0.73 to 1.45 raises the final loss factor from 0.079 to 0.101. The average structure-development rates fluctuate and finish lower, although the discussion says they increased significantly and goes on to describe the gels as more solid-like.[6] The table does not support those directional claims: it shows a greater relative viscous contribution at higher R, and no consistent rise in the average development rate. This calcium-series result should be kept separate from the direct comparison between the two pectin types.
 
-The 2017 paper also illustrates why a supplier shorthand should not replace a formulation trial. Its Table 2 shows the final loss factor rising from 0.079 to 0.101 as R rises from 0.73 to 1.45; meanwhile, the reported average structure-development rate fluctuates rather than rising consistently, despite a sentence in the discussion saying that it increased.[6] We therefore do not use that sentence to claim faster overall structuring at higher calcium. The useful distinction is that **the start of setting and the texture of the cooled gel are separate responses**.
+For development, compare the candidate and incumbent over the calcium and pH range expected in the formula. Keep total solids and the heating/cooling programme fixed in the first comparison. Record the onset of structure, flow during filling, cooled texture and water release. This is a suggested screening design rather than a replication of the paper's recipe.
 
-For development, test the proposed replacement alongside the incumbent across the calcium and pH range the formula will actually encounter. Keep total solids and the heating/cooling programme fixed in that first comparison. Record when structure becomes apparent, how the batch pours or fills, and the cooled product's texture and water release. This is a proposed screening design, not a reproduced recipe from the paper.
+If a candidate forms particles or loses spreadability at another calcium level, check its reaction range and addition procedure before increasing the pectin dose. Product-specific food-safety, preservation and shelf-life validation remain separate from this texture screening.
 
-If the same powder passes at one calcium level but develops particles or loses spreadability at another, the first question should be whether the grade's reaction window fits the process—not whether to increase pectin indiscriminately.
+## Acidified milk drinks need protein stabilisation
 
-## Why the same logic leads to HM in a calcium-containing drink
+H&F lists Classic CM 203 as a protective colloid for low-viscosity fermented milk drinks, alongside other grades intended for gels and yoghurt texture.[11] That application distinction matters when a low-sugar drink needs to remain thin.
 
-A calcium-containing acidified milk drink is not simply a dilute low-sugar jelly. HM pectin can provide protein protection rather than a spoonable calcium-set network. The supplier brochure accordingly lists Classic CM 203 for low-viscosity fermented milk drinks, while its low-DE and amidated grades include gel and yoghurt-texture applications.[11]
+In an original study of directly acidified protein drinks, Liu and colleagues used an experimental [CP Kelco HM pectin reported in the study](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC7723220/fullTextXML) with DE 69%. The pectin-only formulations contained 0.20% and 0.40% w/w pectin.[13] All samples were adjusted to pH 4.0, homogenised at 180–200 bar, heated at 121°C for 4 seconds and cooled. Instrumental and sensory measurements took place within one week.[13]
 
-An original beverage study by Liu and colleagues used an experimental CP Kelco HM pectin with DE 69%, including pectin-only drinks at 0.20% and 0.40% w/w.[13] All samples were adjusted to pH 4.0, homogenised at 180–200 bar, heated at 121°C for 4 seconds and cooled; instrumental and sensory measurements were made within one week.[13] The pectin-only samples were among the lowest-viscosity systems studied, and increasing pectin concentration reduced the measured particle sizes in the pectin-containing samples.[13]
+The authors identified pectin-only and CMC A formulations as the lowest-viscosity systems in their comparison. They also reported smaller particle sizes at higher stabiliser concentrations in pectin-added samples, while describing particle-size changes across all systems as minor.[13]
 
-This is evidence for an acidified protein-drink function, not a calcium-fortification trial. The pectin was experimental, and the paper does not identify its botanical source in the materials description.[13] It cannot validate a particular commercial citrus grade, extra calcium loading or months of shelf life.
+This study supports a protein-drink application under the reported conditions. It did not test calcium fortification, and the materials description does not identify the experimental pectin's botanical source.[13] A commercial citrus grade, added-calcium formulation or longer shelf life would require its own evaluation. The reported heat treatment is an experimental condition, not a validated safety process for another drink.
 
-There is a further purchasing trap: **HM does not mean calcium-inert.** H&F's protein-stabilisation technical guide explains that blockwise free carboxyl groups can give HM citrus pectins calcium reactivity, observable as increased viscosity after adding calcium.[12] The guide specifically advises adding pectin and calcium separately in calcium-enriched acidified drinks, and notes that lower calcium-reactive apple pectins can be advantageous in that setting.[12]
+For calcium-enriched drinks, H&F's [protein-stabilisation guide](https://www.herbstreith-fox.de/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/AWT_Ue_Acidified_milk_products_protein_stabilisation_en.pdf) identifies an additional consideration. Blockwise free carboxyl groups can make HM citrus pectins calcium-reactive, with increased solution viscosity after calcium addition. The guide recommends adding pectin and calcium separately and notes that less calcium-reactive apple pectins can be advantageous in this application.[12]
 
-For a drink that must remain thin, this changes the sourcing brief. Request a protein-stabilising grade evaluated against the actual calcium salt, protein system and heat treatment; do not automatically choose the highest calcium-reactivity LM grade because the drink is low in sugar. A protein-free fortified juice is another task again: the casein-protection evidence above does not establish its stability.
+A thin protein drink therefore needs a grade evaluated with its actual calcium salt, protein system and heat treatment. For a protein-free fortified juice, the selection would need evidence for that formulation rather than casein protection.
 
-## Write the sample request around the failure you cannot accept
+## A sample brief that supports a useful comparison
 
-For a low-sugar fruit spread, a useful brief could read:
+For a reduced-solids fruit spread, a sample request could read:
 
-> We are replacing a citrus pectin in a reduced-solids fruit spread. Please propose a non-amidated LM and, where suitable, an amidated LM candidate for our stated final pH, soluble solids and calcium system. We need sufficient flow through filling followed by the specified spreadable texture, without unacceptable particles or water release. Please provide the complete grade name, DE and DA ranges, standardisation ingredients and the application method used to characterise calcium reactivity and gel performance.
+> Please propose non-amidated LM and, where suitable, amidated LM citrus pectins for the attached final pH, soluble-solids level and calcium system. The product must flow through filling and develop the specified spreadable texture on cooling, with acceptable particle formation and water release. Please provide the full grade name, DE and DA ranges, standardisation ingredients, and the application method used to characterise calcium reactivity and gel performance.
 
-Attach the current process temperatures, residence times and cooling history, plus the fruit base and calcium salt. Ask the supplier to distinguish tests on the supplied powder from tests normalised to pectin content. The purpose is to make two samples comparable, not to accumulate certificates that do not describe setting behaviour.
+Attach the fruit base, calcium salt, process temperatures, residence times and cooling history. Request results on a clearly stated supplied-powder or pectin-content basis so that the samples can be compared consistently.
 
-For an acidified protein drink, replace that gel brief with the actual protein content, calcium addition, acidification route, homogenisation and thermal treatment, and the required drinking viscosity. H&F identifies these formulation and process variables as relevant to pectin stabilisation.[12] Evaluate sediment and particle condition alongside viscosity: a thicker drink is not automatically a better-stabilised one.[12]
+For an acidified protein drink, specify protein content, calcium addition, acidification route, homogenisation, heat treatment and drinking viscosity. H&F identifies these as relevant formulation and process variables, and assesses stability using viscosity, sediment after defined centrifugation and microscopic examination of protein particles.[12]
 
-The practical distinction is simple: **buy a controlled setting window for a low-sugar gel, and a validated stabilisation function for a drink.** HM, LM and DA narrow the choices; they do not finish the selection.
+The final selection should link the complete grade specification to observed performance in the intended process: filling and cooled texture for the spread, or particle stability and drinking viscosity for the protein drink.
 
